<Base
  title="About"
  description="Abhinav Gupta is a full-stack software engineer building backend platforms and browser tooling, with security experience. Roles at Zscaler and SquareX, selected projects, and skills."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I'm a full-stack software engineer. Most of my work is backend platform
      services and browser tooling: auth, data pipelines, Chromium and extension
      features, and the build machinery that keeps them shipping. Security came
      with the territory, so I design with it in mind. I'm open to new roles.
    </p>
  </header>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Zscaler <span class="meta" style="display:inline">(Feb 2026 – Jul 2026)</span></h2>
    <p>
      SquareX was acquired by Zscaler, and I carried the same platform and Zero
      Trust Browser work forward into Zscaler's larger enterprise customer base.
      I rebuilt the Chromium fork's patch-management pipeline so it could keep
      absorbing major upstream upgrades — it's now carried the browser through
      7 major Chromium versions — and shipped 56+ custom Chrome APIs for device
      compliance and threat detection. I also doubled throughput on the browser
      extension's security engine with a sub-millisecond pattern-matching
      rewrite and cut per-tab memory use by 30%, and ported the full browser
      protection stack to Firefox on Android.
    </p>

    <h2>SquareX <span class="meta" style="display:inline">(Jun 2024 – Feb 2026)</span></h2>
    <p>
      I joined as a summer intern and stayed on as a software engineer,
      building backend and browser-protection platform features used by 80+
      enterprise tenants and 40,000+ concurrent users. That included the
      platform's OIDC authentication layer, an enterprise data-leak-protection
      service for Office and PDF files (later ported to WASM for speed), and
      the Zero Trust Browser's UI and content-isolation logic in Chromium. I
      also co-created <a href="https://browser.security">browser.security</a>,
      an open-source framework simulating 30+ browser-security evasion
      techniques, shown at DEF CON and covered by Forbes.
    </p>

    <h2>Selected projects</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/Navknight/rituals">Rituals</a> — a
        cross-platform habit tracker with peer-to-peer image sharing, so
        accountability photos sync without a central server.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/Navknight/zipzap">ZipZap</a> — a WASM zip
        library for in-browser zip extraction and append, cutting client-side
        memory use by over 80% with additive compression.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/navknight/mgpusim">DAP: Dead-Block Aware Prefetching</a>
        — hardware prefetcher modules for multi-GPU systems in MGPUsim,
        cutting cache miss rates by 32%.
      </li>
      <li>
        Parallel sparse tensor decomposition (TTMc) — a tensor-times-matrix
        chain algorithm in the
        <a href="https://github.com/cyclops-community/ctf">Cyclops Tensor Framework</a>,
        accelerated 11x with BLAS and OpenMP.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://browser.security">browser.security</a> — an
        open-source toolkit for probing browser security defenses, presented
        at DEF CON.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Skills</h2>
    <p>
      C/C++, Python, Go, Rust, TypeScript, distributed systems and
      microservices, Chromium/Firefox internals, Kafka, Redis, PostgreSQL,
      GCP, and Kubernetes.
    </p>

    <h2>Education</h2>
    <p>Indian Institute of Technology, Tirupati — B.Tech, Computer Science & Engineering.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
